From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Open Arc Authors <dev@openarc.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 22:03:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] openarc: embedder: the Chrome Web Store names Open Arc
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Plan B61 (docs/arc-spec/06-flows.md §18.2, §18.3): the Chrome Web Store
shows every browser that is not Google Chrome a "Switch to Chrome?" prompt,
a "Switch to Chrome to install extensions and themes" banner and an
"Add to Chrome" button. The server decides this from data of its own, not
from the user agent or the client hint brands, so sending other brands
would not help (and would break sites). The spec records the reference
browser rewriting the page after load instead.

openarc/browser/openarc_web_store_branding.{h,cc}: WebStoreBranding, a
per-tab WebContentsObserver attached with the other tab helpers. When the
primary main frame's document element of an https page on the store's
domain (extension_urls::IsWebstoreDomain) becomes available, it runs a
script in Chrome's internal isolated world (the page cannot see or undo
it) that:
- renames "Add to Chrome" / "Remove from Chrome" to "Add to Open Arc" /
  "Remove from Open Arc", text and aria-labels;
- hides the banner's component and the prompt's dialog;
- keeps doing so for nodes the store's single-page app adds later
  (MutationObserver on the document).
Texts are matched whole; if the store changes its strings the page shows
Chrome's wording again. Installing is unchanged: the button drives
chrome.webstorePrivate, which our profiles have (C14).
